<h2 id="mean-curves">Descriptive mean curves</h2>
<p>The dashed gold curve estimates the mean of reported model configurations within one source, metric, benchmark version and task condition. It does not pool native scores across benchmarks. All individual points remain visible. Repeated results for the same named configuration and release are averaged first, giving each configuration one weight. Human references, approximate figure readings and undated results do not enter the fit.</p>
<p>We use a Gaussian-kernel regression with a fixed bandwidth of 180 days. A curve requires at least five dated configurations, four distinct release-date labels and a span of 90 days. It is drawn only between the first and last observed release dates, with local effective sample size of at least two configurations and an observation within one bandwidth. Longer unsupported gaps remain open. Where these criteria are not met, the figure states why no curve is fitted.</p>
<p>This is a descriptive mean of the selected published configurations. Changing model composition, model size, training, prompting, contamination, evaluation timing and other differences can explain changes over time. The curve estimates neither a causal effect of calendar time nor the performance of a representative population of all available models. No confidence band is invented from this heterogeneous convenience sample.</p>
<details><summary>Calculation and sensitivity</summary>
<p>At date t, the mean is the sum of w_i(t) y_i divided by the sum of w_i(t), with w_i(t) = exp(−0.5 ((t_i − t) / 180 days)²). Month-only releases use the same interval midpoint as their plotted mark; the full month remains visible. Identical date/score marks may overlap, but their original inputs remain separate unless they repeat the same configuration.</p>
<p>Alternative bandwidths of 90 and 365 days are retained as sensitivity calculations. They are not confidence intervals. The complete inputs, evaluation grid and fitting decisions are downloadable: <a href="data/mean_curve_inputs.csv">inputs with observation IDs</a>, <a href="data/mean_curve_grid.csv">all three smoothing grids</a>, <a href="data/mean_curve_summary.csv">decisions for every condition</a>, <a href="data/mean_curve_method.json">method specification</a>.</p></details>
<p>Current coverage: {{CURVE_COUNT}} source/task conditions in {{CURVE_FAMILIES}} benchmark families support a curve. A missing curve is not evidence that performance has remained unchanged.</p>
